# Evals & Scaffold Rules

## Evals (Braintrust and equivalents)

- Golden datasets should live in the repo (or have a documented export path) so another engineer can run them.
- Prefer scorers that catch real regressions: tool choice, schema validity, output shape, compile success.
- Use custom scorers when autoevals are not enough; keep scorer logic reviewable.
- Every meaningful change to tool behavior or scaffold output should consider whether an eval case needs to be added or updated.
- Checked-in experiment summaries in `docs/` are part of the product narrative, not optional notes.

## Scaffolding

- Scaffold from an explicit schema or resource, not from ad-hoc prompts alone.
- Output should be typed, reviewable, and preferably compilable (or structured enough that compile/structure checks are meaningful).
- Prefer narrow, useful scaffolds (typed client, page stub, studio stub) over generating an entire application.
- Scaffold tools exposed over MCP should share contracts with the CLI scaffold path.

## Repair loops

- A repair loop needs a clear feedback signal (compile error, test failure, schema mismatch) and a bounded patch step.
- Do not invent open-ended “keep fixing forever” loops without stop conditions.
- Eval coverage for “output compiles / matches golden structure” is more valuable than aspirational multi-step agent autonomy.

## Quality bar

- Headless tools + evals come before polished UI.
- If a scaffold or tool change cannot be described in an eval or a short postmortem, reconsider whether it belongs in the current phase.
